Docker Pull

Sytax : docker pull [OPTIONS] IMAGE[:TAG|@DIGEST]

docker pull ubuntu
docker pull ubuntu:20.04
docker pull --all-tags ubuntu

Docker Images

Syntax : docker images [OPTIONS]
docker images
docker images -q

Docker PS

Syntax : docker ps[OPTIONS]

docker ps
docker ps -a / --all

Docker Start and stop

docker start containerName/containerId
docker start containerName/containerId

Docker exec : 

Used to interact with the container

docker exec my-container ls /usr/src/app

options : 
i - for interactive , keeps container stdin open, allow you to send the input

docker exec -i my-container cat /etc/hostname

Opening a terminal inside terminal

docker -i -t my-container /bin/bash
docker -it my-container /bin/bash

To remove a container:
docker rm <container_id_or_name>

To remove a image:
docker rmi <image_id_or_name>


If you want to clean up everything in one go:


docker container prune   # removes all stopped containers
docker image prune       # removes unused images


Docker Run

docker run = docker pull + docker create + docker start
docker run --name cont1 -it ubuntu:latest /bin/bash

First, it will try to locate image locally, if not avaialble, it will pull from the docker hub, and then start the same.

docker run --rm ubuntu echo "Test"
create and run container + echo test + then delete it

Use it like one time


docker inspect
